.TH "TDDA CAT" 1 "May 2026" "3.0" "tdda cat manual"
.SH NAME
\fBtdda cat\fR — Display rows from a dataset as a rich table

.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBtdda cat\fR [\fB-h\fR] [\fBN\fR | \fB-N\fR | \fB+N\fR] [\fB-s\fR | \fB-S\fR]
           [\fB--fields\fR \fIFIELDS\fP] [\fB--xfields\fR \fIFIELDS\fP]
           [\fB-r\fR \fIN\fP [\fB--seed\fR \fISEED\fP]]
           [\fB--pandas\fR] [\fB--polars\fR] [\fB--backend\fR \fIBACKEND\fP]
           \fIINPUT\fP [\fIFIELD\fP ...]

.SH POSITIONAL ARGUMENTS
\fIINPUT\fP is one of:
  - a CSV file (or \fB\&.tsv\fR, \fB\&.psv\fR, \fB\&.txt\fR)
  - a Parquet file (\fB\&.parquet\fR)
  - a flat file with colon syntax to trigger metadata lookup
    (e.g. \fBfoo.csv:\fR)
  - a flat file with an explicit metadata path
    (e.g. \fBfoo.csv:foo.serial\fR)

.PP
\fIFIELD\fP ...  Field names (or \fBfnmatch\fR wildcard patterns) to display.
           Fields appear in the order given. Equivalent to \fB--fields\fR;
           both may be combined. Wildcards must be quoted in the shell.

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBtdda cat\fR command displays rows from a dataset as a rich table.

.PP
Without a row count, all rows are shown.

.PP
\fBN\fR or \fB-N\fR    First N rows
.br
  \fB+N\fR           Last N rows

.PP
Null values are shown as \fB∅\fR\&.

.SH OPTIONS
\fB-h\fR, \fB-?\fR, \fB--help\fR        Show this help message and exit

.PP
\fB--fields\fR \fIFIELDS\fP         Show only these fields. \fIFIELDS\fP is a
                          comma- or space-separated list of field names
                          or \fBfnmatch\fR wildcard patterns (e.g. \fBeu_*\fR,
                          \fB[a-z]*\fR). Fields appear in the order
                          specified. Requires quoting in the shell when
                          using spaces or wildcards.

.PP
\fB--xfields\fR \fIFIELDS\fP        Exclude these fields. Same format as
                          \fB--fields\fR\&. Fields appear in dataset order.

.PP
\fB-s\fR                        Short headers: column width driven by data;
                          headers split at word boundaries (punctuation
                          and lowercase→uppercase transitions) and packed
                          onto as few lines as possible.

.PP
\fB-S\fR                        Short headers: as \fB-s\fR but split anywhere
                          (mid-word) to fit the data width.

.PP
\fB-r\fR \fIN\fP, \fB--random\fR \fIN\fP      Show \fIN\fP random rows instead of a slice.

.PP
\fB--seed\fR \fISEED\fP             Random seed for \fB-r\fR\&. If omitted, a seed is
                          chosen automatically and printed.

.PP
\fB--pandas\fR, \fB--pd\fR          Use Pandas as DataFrame engine (default)
.br
\fB--polars\fR, \fB--pl\fR          Use Polars as DataFrame engine
.br
\fB--backend\fR, \fB-B\fR \fIBACKEND\fP   Backend choice for Pandas
.br
                          \fBn\fR for numpy_nullable *
.br
                          \fBa\fR for pyarrow
.br
                          \fBo\fR for original

.SH EXAMPLES
1) \fBtdda cat accounts1k.parquet\fR

.PP
Display all rows from \fBaccounts1k.parquet\fR\&.

.PP
2) \fBtdda cat -10 accounts1k.csv:\fR

.PP
Display the first 10 rows, using any associated metadata file.

.PP
3) \fBtdda cat +10 accounts1k.csv:\fR

.PP
Display the last 10 rows.

.PP
4) \fBtdda cat --fields 'name,balance' accounts1k.csv:\fR

.PP
Display only the \fBname\fR and \fBbalance\fR fields.

.PP
5) \fBtdda cat --fields 'amount*' --xfields '*_raw' accounts1k.csv:\fR

.PP
Display fields matching \fBamount*\fR, excluding those ending in \fB_raw\fR\&.

.PP
6) \fBtdda cat -r 20 --seed 42 accounts1k.csv:\fR

.PP
Display 20 random rows with a fixed seed.

.PP
7) \fBtdda cat -s accounts1k.csv:\fR

.PP
Display all rows with compact multi-line headers, splitting at word
boundaries (\fBopen_date\fR → \fBopen date\fR, \fBaccountType\fR → \fBaccount Type\fR).

